WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.275
<v Alex>Picture this. You ask an A I assistant to build something for you.

2
00:00:05.275 --> 00:00:08.927
<v Alex>And when it finishes, the result is completely wrong.

3
00:00:09.502 --> 00:00:13.980
<v Maya>So what do you check first? The model? The settings?

4
00:00:14.610 --> 00:00:17.718
<v Alex>Neither. You go back and read your own instruction.

5
00:00:17.718 --> 00:00:22.899
<v Alex>Because most of the time, the problem is right there, in what you asked for.

6
00:00:23.504 --> 00:00:26.153
<v Maya>Even the strongest model cannot read your mind.

7
00:00:26.153 --> 00:00:31.450
<v Maya>There is always a gap between what you want and what the assistant thinks you want.

8
00:00:31.945 --> 00:00:39.049
<v Alex>Today we talk about why the most valuable skill in twenty twenty-seven is simply saying clearly what you want.

9
00:00:48.149 --> 00:00:54.491
<v Alex>Welcome to The Daily Signal. Your daily download on A I, growth, and building things.

10
00:00:55.101 --> 00:00:56.269
<v Alex>I'm Alex.

11
00:00:56.894 --> 00:01:02.392
<v Maya>And I'm Maya. It's Saturday, October seventeenth, twenty twenty-six.

12
00:01:02.843 --> 00:01:03.843
<v Alex>Happy Saturday, Fisher.

13
00:01:03.843 --> 00:01:08.510
<v Alex>Before we jump in, a quick note on how today's notes were gathered.

14
00:01:09.124 --> 00:01:14.841
<v Maya>Everything in today's show comes from Fisher's personal X home timeline.

15
00:01:14.841 --> 00:01:20.118
<v Maya>Specifically, the Following feed, read through his connected account, hawking five twenty.

16
00:01:20.728 --> 00:01:29.711
<v Alex>And following the daily rule, it was read just once early this morning, Beijing time, around six twenty in the morning.

17
00:01:29.711 --> 00:01:36.128
<v Alex>Two pages of posts, covering from twelve nineteen after midnight to six fifteen this morning.

18
00:01:36.671 --> 00:01:42.190
<v Maya>That means posts from yesterday daytime and late last night were not read.

19
00:01:42.190 --> 00:01:48.557
<v Maya>We didn't read a third page. Nothing was liked or replied to. Read only.

20
00:01:49.068 --> 00:01:52.237
<v Alex>A lot of noise was filtered out.

21
00:01:52.237 --> 00:01:58.575
<v Alex>New video model launches, Elon Musk reposts, political threads, and get-rich-quick pitches.

22
00:01:59.080 --> 00:02:04.101
<v Maya>Leaving us with thirteen items, including one borderline item at the end.

23
00:02:04.602 --> 00:02:07.237
<v Alex>Here's what's coming today. One.

24
00:02:07.237 --> 00:02:15.519
<v Alex>How always-on agents structure their memory in four layers, and why saying clearly what you want beats having a newer model.

25
00:02:16.058 --> 00:02:16.466
<v Maya>Two.

26
00:02:16.466 --> 00:02:26.759
<v Maya>Why a post with four thousand views brought in more clients than a post with six hundred thousand views, plus a classic checkout page trick from levelsio.

27
00:02:27.310 --> 00:02:27.684
<v Alex>Three.

28
00:02:27.684 --> 00:02:33.969
<v Alex>What happens when your next website visitor isn't a human, but someone else's A I agent.

29
00:02:34.361 --> 00:02:44.988
<v Maya>简单说：今天讲常驻助手的四层记忆、为什么把话说明白最值钱，以及真能带来客户的增长细节。

30
00:02:47.038 --> 00:02:49.105
<v Alex>Segment one. Top stories.

31
00:02:49.754 --> 00:02:57.518
<v Maya>Our first story comes from an account called r r y s s f, with about forty-one thousand followers on X.

32
00:02:58.127 --> 00:03:07.066
<v Alex>He broke down a forty-five-minute lecture given at Stanford's computer science class, C S one four six S, by Lee Robinson.

33
00:03:07.640 --> 00:03:11.599
<v Maya>In this lecture, he explained the structure behind Grok Bot.

34
00:03:11.599 --> 00:03:15.557
<v Maya>Basically, how you build an always-on A I agent.

35
00:03:16.205 --> 00:03:22.602
<v Alex>"Always-on" sounds like a computer running twenty-four hours a day, burning electricity.

36
00:03:23.095 --> 00:03:25.712
<v Maya>Right, that's what most people think.

37
00:03:25.712 --> 00:03:31.695
<v Maya>But point number one from the lecture is that always-on does not mean running nonstop.

38
00:03:31.695 --> 00:03:33.939
<v Maya>Servers and computers go to sleep.

39
00:03:33.939 --> 00:03:39.174
<v Maya>The agent only wakes up when a message arrives or a timer triggers it.

40
00:03:39.656 --> 00:03:43.852
<v Alex>Okay. That makes a lot more sense. What happens when it wakes up?

41
00:03:43.852 --> 00:03:46.434
<v Alex>How does it remember what it was doing?

42
00:03:47.006 --> 00:03:51.062
<v Maya>That's point number two: memory is split into four layers.

43
00:03:51.521 --> 00:03:53.651
<v Alex>Four layers. Walk me through them.

44
00:03:54.108 --> 00:03:56.079
<v Maya>Layer one is core facts.

45
00:03:56.079 --> 00:04:01.993
<v Maya>The essential rules and facts the agent carries with it every single time it runs.

46
00:04:02.546 --> 00:04:05.037
<v Alex>Like a permanent ID card and rulebook.

47
00:04:05.580 --> 00:04:10.358
<v Maya>Exactly. Layer two is a daily conversation log, organized by date.

48
00:04:10.358 --> 00:04:15.136
<v Maya>Layer three is scratch notes and drafts that expire over time.

49
00:04:15.136 --> 00:04:24.691
<v Maya>And layer four is everything else—kept in regular files that the agent only searches when it needs to look something up.

50
00:04:25.325 --> 00:04:26.773
<v Alex>That is super clean.

51
00:04:26.773 --> 00:04:32.929
<v Alex>Because if you put everything into layer one, the agent gets confused or runs out of space.

52
00:04:33.331 --> 00:04:43.531
<v Maya>也就是说：把记忆分成核心事实、每日日志、临时草稿和档案库四层，别一股脑全塞给助手。

53
00:04:44.084 --> 00:04:47.202
<v Alex>What else did Lee Robinson cover in the Stanford talk?

54
00:04:47.751 --> 00:04:51.336
<v Maya>Point three is the order of asking for help.

55
00:04:51.336 --> 00:04:54.920
<v Maya>And it goes strictly from cheapest to most expensive.

56
00:04:55.420 --> 00:04:58.098
<v Alex>Oh, I like this. How does the ladder work?

57
00:04:58.550 --> 00:05:02.733
<v Maya>First, check what the agent already knows in its files.

58
00:05:02.733 --> 00:05:11.517
<v Maya>Second, use a plugin or an A P I—an A P I is a direct data connection between software tools.

59
00:05:11.517 --> 00:05:18.209
<v Maya>Third, do a web search. Fourth, open a web browser. Fifth, use the full computer desktop.

60
00:05:18.209 --> 00:05:23.228
<v Maya>And sixth, at the very end of the line: ask the human.

61
00:05:23.717 --> 00:05:25.170
<v Alex>Ask the human last!

62
00:05:25.170 --> 00:05:31.346
<v Alex>Most people set up their bots backwards, where the bot asks you a question every two minutes.

63
00:05:31.934 --> 00:05:36.122
<v Maya>And point four is about how the team of agents works.

64
00:05:36.122 --> 00:05:39.168
<v Maya>The main agent only acts as a dispatcher.

65
00:05:39.168 --> 00:05:46.402
<v Maya>It hands the messy, detailed work to sub-agents, and those sub-agents only bring back the final result.

66
00:05:46.892 --> 00:05:49.353
<v Alex>So the main agent's workspace stays clean.

67
00:05:49.877 --> 00:05:55.492
<v Maya>Now, at almost the exact same time, another big account posted the opposite view.

68
00:05:55.492 --> 00:05:57.096
<v Maya>That's Uncle Bob.

69
00:05:57.096 --> 00:06:02.310
<v Maya>A big account in software, with about two hundred eighty-five thousand followers.

70
00:06:02.761 --> 00:06:04.027
<v Alex>What did Uncle Bob say?

71
00:06:04.643 --> 00:06:07.128
<v Maya>His post got three hundred twelve saves.

72
00:06:07.128 --> 00:06:15.291
<v Maya>He said as models get stronger, a single disciplined agent can handle big jobs that used to require a whole swarm of agents.

73
00:06:15.772 --> 00:06:16.785
<v Alex>Interesting contrast.

74
00:06:16.785 --> 00:06:22.867
<v Alex>One dispatcher with sub-agents versus one disciplined agent doing it all.

75
00:06:23.371 --> 00:06:26.419
<v Maya>Why does this matter so much to Fisher?

76
00:06:26.419 --> 00:06:33.278
<v Maya>Because Fisher's own three-assistant team matches Lee Robinson's four-layer structure almost one-to-one.

77
00:06:33.904 --> 00:06:35.714
<v Alex>Let's map that out.

78
00:06:35.714 --> 00:06:41.145
<v Alex>In Fisher's setup, the file called memory dot M D holds the core facts.

79
00:06:41.145 --> 00:06:46.214
<v Alex>The file called chat dot M D is the daily log organized by date.

80
00:06:46.214 --> 00:06:54.179
<v Alex>And a file called state dot J S O N acts as a lock to control which assistant is running right now.

81
00:06:54.730 --> 00:06:58.662
<v Maya>Exactly. Fisher didn't copy a Stanford lecture to build that.

82
00:06:58.662 --> 00:07:02.236
<v Maya>He built it by hitting real walls and fixing them.

83
00:07:02.236 --> 00:07:06.883
<v Maya>So this lecture gives him an outside reference with a name on it.

84
00:07:06.883 --> 00:07:11.887
<v Maya>It shows that other people are solving the same problems in the same way.

85
00:07:12.507 --> 00:07:18.064
<v Alex>And why does Fisher still use three assistants instead of Uncle Bob's single agent?

86
00:07:18.642 --> 00:07:27.198
<v Maya>That's in his library item E V oh eight, which records how his three-bot system works and how they catch mistakes.

87
00:07:27.198 --> 00:07:30.918
<v Maya>For Fisher, having multiple assistants isn't about raw power.

88
00:07:30.918 --> 00:07:33.150
<v Maya>It's about checks and balances.

89
00:07:33.150 --> 00:07:37.987
<v Maya>One assistant is the Owner bot whose job is to check the others.

90
00:07:38.585 --> 00:07:52.853
<v Alex>For a writing angle, the daily notes suggest combining library item E V oh eight with topic W M P six a—mistakes learned from running multiple A I assistants at once.

91
00:07:52.853 --> 00:08:04.446
<v Alex>He can write about why his assistants split memory into layers, comparing his own files to the Stanford lecture without getting bogged down in technical jargon.

92
00:08:04.914 --> 00:08:16.246
<v Maya>Plus, the link to Lee Robinson's lecture is well worth Fisher listening to himself, and we have drafts to reply to both r r y s s f and Uncle Bob later in the show.

93
00:08:16.804 --> 00:08:26.505
<v Alex>So the takeaway is: layer your agent's memory by how often it changes, and make asking the human the last step, not the first.

94
00:08:27.491 --> 00:08:35.371
<v Maya>一句话：把助手的记忆按冷热分层，能自己查的先自己查，最后才问人。

95
00:08:37.421 --> 00:08:41.232
<v Alex>Story number two connects right to our cold open.

96
00:08:41.232 --> 00:08:47.583
<v Alex>This comes from Nate Herk, who has about thirty-six thousand followers, interviewing Matt Pocock.

97
00:08:48.208 --> 00:08:55.142
<v Maya>Nate asked Matt a simple question: What will be the most valuable A I skill in twenty twenty-seven?

98
00:08:55.665 --> 00:09:00.434
<v Alex>People probably expect him to say some new tool or some clever prompting trick.

99
00:09:01.004 --> 00:09:03.165
<v Maya>Instead, Matt said: communication.

100
00:09:03.165 --> 00:09:09.109
<v Maya>How clearly you think determines how clearly the thing gets built.

101
00:09:09.571 --> 00:09:14.192
<v Alex>Let that sink in. How clearly you think is how clearly it gets built.

102
00:09:14.720 --> 00:09:20.521
<v Maya>He explained that no matter how strong the model gets, it cannot read what is inside your head.

103
00:09:20.521 --> 00:09:25.678
<v Maya>There is always a gap between what you want and what the agent thinks you want.

104
00:09:26.193 --> 00:09:27.858
<v Alex>And how does Matt close that gap?

105
00:09:28.338 --> 00:09:31.690
<v Maya>He shared a skill he uses called grill-me.

106
00:09:31.690 --> 00:09:38.395
<v Maya>Before the agent starts building anything, grill-me tells the agent to turn around and interrogate you first.

107
00:09:39.008 --> 00:09:41.913
<v Alex>Ha! The assistant interviews the boss.

108
00:09:42.439 --> 00:09:49.200
<v Maya>Yes! It asks you the hard questions early. Things like safety, and how it will go live.

109
00:09:49.200 --> 00:09:51.188
<v Maya>All before it starts building.

110
00:09:51.188 --> 00:10:02.324
<v Maya>And Nate Herk added a great line right after: a clear communicator using an older model will get better results than a vague communicator using the newest model.

111
00:10:03.070 --> 00:10:15.183
<v Maya>简单说：脑子里想得多清楚，A I 才做得出来多清楚；会表达的人用旧模型，也比说不清的人用新模型产出好。

112
00:10:15.751 --> 00:10:20.637
<v Alex>Now let's connect this to Fisher. Remember, Fisher does not write code.

113
00:10:20.637 --> 00:10:23.644
<v Alex>Everything he builds, he builds with A I.

114
00:10:24.215 --> 00:10:30.740
<v Maya>And that's why this story plays to his strength. For years, Fisher managed human teams.

115
00:10:30.740 --> 00:10:38.896
<v Maya>Writing requirements, defining goals, writing handover notes—all of that is literally the skill of saying clearly what you want.

116
00:10:39.474 --> 00:10:44.267
<v Alex>People who managed teams know this. You have to say what "done" looks like first.

117
00:10:44.852 --> 00:10:59.260
<v Maya>The daily notes point directly to Fisher's library items D R oh one—"I don't write code, how I get A I to work"—and W M P one b—"I don't write code, what I learned shipping with A I."

118
00:10:59.740 --> 00:11:05.209
<v Alex>The angle is simple: what advantage does a non-coder have when working with A I?

119
00:11:05.209 --> 00:11:09.995
<v Alex>And we must be careful never to frame it as if he writes code.

120
00:11:09.995 --> 00:11:20.250
<v Alex>In fact, this is so strong that our daily notes picked it as today's number one original post idea, and we also have a reply draft for Nate Herk.

121
00:11:20.788 --> 00:11:27.179
<v Maya>So the takeaway is: before you blame the model for a bad result, read your own instructions first.

122
00:11:28.377 --> 00:11:36.743
<v Maya>一句话：不写代码的人跟 A I 合作，最大的本事就是像带团队一样把需求说清楚。

123
00:11:38.793 --> 00:11:44.672
<v Alex>Let's bring in story ten right here, because it's also about managing agents day-to-day.

124
00:11:44.672 --> 00:11:48.919
<v Alex>It's from Ben Tossell, who has about two hundred two thousand followers.

125
00:11:49.449 --> 00:11:55.413
<v Maya>We talked about Ben yesterday when he asked whether people use one thread or many agents.

126
00:11:55.413 --> 00:12:00.258
<v Maya>Today, he posted that he spent all day working on his agent setup.

127
00:12:00.591 --> 00:12:01.339
<v Alex>What did he build?

128
00:12:01.983 --> 00:12:06.209
<v Maya>His bot now spins up separate threads for formal coding tasks.

129
00:12:06.209 --> 00:12:13.894
<v Maya>Then those threads send email updates back to the main bot, and update a shared task table and work log.

130
00:12:14.387 --> 00:12:18.689
<v Alex>Wait. Threads sending emails to a bot? How did that go?

131
00:12:19.273 --> 00:12:24.495
<v Maya>In a second post right after, Ben complained that his bot isn't checking its own email!

132
00:12:25.005 --> 00:12:29.269
<v Alex>Okay, that is pretty funny. Even A I bots ignore their inbox now.

133
00:12:29.894 --> 00:12:35.184
<v Maya>It's funny, but it shows a real problem that Fisher's team already solved.

134
00:12:35.184 --> 00:12:40.827
<v Maya>If you rely on agents to check messages or emails on their own, things get dropped.

135
00:12:41.410 --> 00:12:43.239
<v Alex>How does Fisher's team handle it?

136
00:12:43.715 --> 00:13:01.254
<v Maya>That's in library item E V oh eight and rule D R oh five: every file has only one writer, and for the shared group log, every assistant must read the last fifty lines before it starts any job, and append just one line at the very end when it finishes.

137
00:13:01.873 --> 00:13:03.870
<v Alex>No email inbox to check.

138
00:13:03.870 --> 00:13:09.862
<v Alex>Just one shared log file, read the tail before starting, add one line when done.

139
00:13:10.305 --> 00:13:22.957
<v Maya>也就是说：别指望助手自己去查收件箱，规定它开工前必须读共享日志的最后几行，做完在末尾加一行，最稳当。

140
00:13:23.587 --> 00:13:30.776
<v Alex>For writing, the notes say don't write a standalone post on this, since we replied to Ben yesterday too.

141
00:13:30.776 --> 00:13:35.090
<v Alex>If Fisher replies today, he can share that shared-log angle instead.

142
00:13:35.654 --> 00:13:43.468
<v Maya>So the takeaway is: a boring rule that runs every time beats a clever message system that an agent forgets to check.

143
00:13:44.647 --> 00:13:51.607
<v Maya>一句话：固定读写同一份日志，比让助手自己收邮件靠谱得多。

144
00:13:53.657 --> 00:13:58.513
<v Alex>One more story in this segment on controlling what A I produces.

145
00:13:58.513 --> 00:14:06.607
<v Alex>This is story eleven, from a Chinese account on X called yucheng, based in Boston, with about twenty thousand followers.

146
00:14:07.095 --> 00:14:15.396
<v Maya>He asked a question in Chinese that only got ten replies so far, which makes it a great place to chime in.

147
00:14:15.396 --> 00:14:21.433
<v Maya>He asked: Claude Code keeps putting casual, chatty explanations directly onto the web page it builds.

148
00:14:22.069 --> 00:14:29.573
<v Alex>Wait—so the website itself ends up reading like developer notes instead of a real product for users?

149
00:14:30.133 --> 00:14:30.606
<v Maya>Exactly!

150
00:14:30.606 --> 00:14:37.223
<v Maya>Instead of clean product text, the page has conversational filler written right into the layout.

151
00:14:37.223 --> 00:14:40.752
<v Maya>He's asking how people constrain that behavior.

152
00:14:41.238 --> 00:14:49.842
<v Alex>That sounds a lot like Story three from yesterday's episode—where Shann Holmberg talked about naming the exact design patterns you don't want.

153
00:14:50.469 --> 00:14:54.513
<v Maya>It is the exact same problem, just in words instead of visuals.

154
00:14:54.513 --> 00:14:58.558
<v Maya>And Fisher doesn't need to talk about code to answer this.

155
00:14:58.558 --> 00:15:00.917
<v Maya>He already solved this for writing copy.

156
00:15:01.496 --> 00:15:06.690
<v Alex>Right. When you tell an A I "don't sound like A I," it doesn't know what to do.

157
00:15:07.254 --> 00:15:12.178
<v Maya>Fisher already has specific "no A I tone" rules for his own copy.

158
00:15:12.178 --> 00:15:15.208
<v Maya>So the suggested reply is about writing rules.

159
00:15:15.208 --> 00:15:25.434
<v Maya>Put one line in the rules: "Only put text on the page that the user needs to see." Then list the exact phrases you don't want.

160
00:15:26.059 --> 00:15:38.248
<v Maya>简单说：只说“别写口水话”没用，要在规则里写明“页面上只放用户要看的话”，再把不准出现的句式一条条列出来。

161
00:15:38.780 --> 00:15:47.035
<v Alex>The daily notes say no need for a standalone post here, just a helpful Chinese reply sharing that rule-writing approach.

162
00:15:47.533 --> 00:15:56.471
<v Maya>So the takeaway is: whether it's page design or page copy, replace vague complaints with a blacklist of specific examples.

163
00:15:57.629 --> 00:16:04.106
<v Maya>一句话：约束 A I 的口水话，靠的是列出具体的禁用说法。

164
00:16:06.156 --> 00:16:09.924
<v Alex>Segment two. Growth, conversion, and brand.

165
00:16:10.549 --> 00:16:16.736
<v Maya>Let's start with story three, from Marcel Kargul, who has about sixty thousand followers on X.

166
00:16:17.279 --> 00:16:23.959
<v Alex>His point is one every growth person loves: virality does not equal conversion.

167
00:16:24.519 --> 00:16:29.980
<v Maya>Here is the contrast he shared. Over the past few weeks, he had two posts.

168
00:16:29.980 --> 00:16:34.350
<v Maya>One was a visual interface post that got six hundred thousand views.

169
00:16:34.350 --> 00:16:40.539
<v Maya>The other was a case study post that got only four thousand views and one hundred bookmarks.

170
00:16:41.054 --> 00:16:44.468
<v Alex>Six hundred thousand views versus four thousand views.

171
00:16:44.468 --> 00:16:50.015
<v Alex>Most people would celebrate the six hundred thousand and forget the four thousand.

172
00:16:50.615 --> 00:16:54.836
<v Maya>Right. Except he tracks where his paying clients actually come from.

173
00:16:54.836 --> 00:17:04.430
<v Maya>And he says the four-thousand-view case study brought in more clients, and in fact case studies convert better for him every single time.

174
00:17:04.885 --> 00:17:07.904
<v Alex>Now, let's add our standard honesty check here.

175
00:17:08.428 --> 00:17:14.931
<v Maya>Yes. Those numbers are his own claim on X—there is no independent verification.

176
00:17:15.387 --> 00:17:19.641
<v Alex>Even so, the logic matches Fisher's own growth philosophy completely.

177
00:17:19.641 --> 00:17:25.055
<v Alex>Fisher is not on X to chase huge view counts or become an influencer.

178
00:17:25.055 --> 00:17:32.790
<v Alex>He is there so people who want to work together can see real examples of how he thinks and builds.

179
00:17:33.264 --> 00:17:37.208
<v Maya>Think about the difference between a view and a bookmark.

180
00:17:37.208 --> 00:17:43.518
<v Maya>A big view count just tells you the topic was broad enough for strangers scrolling by.

181
00:17:43.518 --> 00:17:49.828
<v Maya>A bookmark—a save—is much closer to someone saying, "I actually want to use this."

182
00:17:50.571 --> 00:17:59.151
<v Maya>也就是说：浏览量只说明话题热闹，收藏数和私信才代表真的有人想用你的方法。

183
00:17:59.732 --> 00:18:05.028
<v Alex>For writing, the notes say don't write a standalone post on this.

184
00:18:05.028 --> 00:18:13.174
<v Alex>Instead, use it as a rule for Fisher's own weekly review: measure bookmarks and direct messages, not just impressions.

185
00:18:13.174 --> 00:18:16.840
<v Alex>And we have a nice reply draft for Marcel.

186
00:18:17.376 --> 00:18:24.421
<v Maya>So the takeaway is: write for the people who will save your case study, not for the crowd that scrolls past.

187
00:18:25.676 --> 00:18:32.874
<v Maya>一句话：看转化要盯收藏和私信，别被几十万的浏览量哄住。

188
00:18:34.924 --> 00:18:37.143
<v Alex>Story four is from levelsio.

189
00:18:37.143 --> 00:18:43.800
<v Alex>That's Pieter Levels, an indie builder with about nine hundred seventy-six thousand followers.

190
00:18:44.424 --> 00:18:49.838
<v Maya>He had three separate posts in the feed this morning. Let's look at the first one.

191
00:18:49.838 --> 00:18:58.118
<v Maya>On the checkout page of one of his products, right underneath the payment box, he added a small link that says: "Don't want to join?

192
00:18:58.118 --> 00:18:59.073
<v Maya>Tell me why."

193
00:18:59.592 --> 00:19:00.768
<v Alex>And when someone clicks it?

194
00:19:01.336 --> 00:19:06.602
<v Maya>A tiny survey modal opens up where they can type why they decided not to pay.

195
00:19:07.188 --> 00:19:10.790
<v Alex>That is such a classic, old-school growth move!

196
00:19:10.790 --> 00:19:16.792
<v Alex>Asking churned or bouncing users why they are leaving right at the moment of friction.

197
00:19:17.313 --> 00:19:19.838
<v Maya>And indie developers are rediscovering it now.

198
00:19:19.838 --> 00:19:22.723
<v Maya>Fisher knows this well from his growth work.

199
00:19:22.723 --> 00:19:29.216
<v Maya>When you ask paying customers for feedback, you're talking to people who already got over the hurdle.

200
00:19:29.216 --> 00:19:36.070
<v Maya>The people who stop at the checkout page are the ones who know what is actually broken or unclear.

201
00:19:36.624 --> 00:19:38.899
<v Alex>What were levelsio's other two posts?

202
00:19:39.502 --> 00:19:50.810
<v Maya>In the second post, which got two thousand one hundred nine bookmarks, he said A I companies are going to eat most software categories, so he is busy chasing the remaining moats.

203
00:19:51.248 --> 00:19:52.121
<v Alex>And the third one?

204
00:19:52.601 --> 00:20:00.698
<v Maya>In the third one, he complained that under one of his tweets, there were more than one hundred fifty A I-generated replies.

205
00:20:00.698 --> 00:20:04.218
<v Maya>He wrote: "A I replies have never been this bad."

206
00:20:04.854 --> 00:20:08.146
<v Alex>One hundred fifty robotic replies under one thread.

207
00:20:08.146 --> 00:20:13.905
<v Alex>That is a huge warning sign for anyone doing outreach or replies on X.

208
00:20:14.356 --> 00:20:29.715
<v Maya>简单说：问放弃结账的人为什么走，是最老派也最管用的增长动作；另外现在 X 上满地都是烂 A I 回复，互动必须写得像真人、有真细节。

209
00:20:30.316 --> 00:20:35.148
<v Alex>What's the writing angle for Fisher on that checkout survey idea?

210
00:20:35.760 --> 00:20:47.033
<v Maya>He can anchor it on library item W M P four b—"Seven users, thirty-one days no signup, what I changed"—or E V oh five, his zero-download app story.

211
00:20:47.033 --> 00:20:55.136
<v Maya>The angle is: when nobody is signing up, the people you need to talk to most are the ones who didn't register.

212
00:20:55.613 --> 00:21:03.555
<v Alex>Plus we have a reply draft for levelsio's checkout post, written in plain human language with zero robotic praise.

213
00:21:04.088 --> 00:21:11.742
<v Maya>So the takeaway is: talk to the people who walked away at the door, and never sound like a generic bot in someone's replies.

214
00:21:13.055 --> 00:21:20.730
<v Maya>一句话：没人买单时先去问离开的人，写回复时千万别带半点机器味。

215
00:21:22.780 --> 00:21:33.302
<v Alex>Let's pair that with story nine, from a much smaller account called m v r c k h c k r, with three hundred forty-two followers.

216
00:21:33.755 --> 00:21:39.865
<v Maya>Even though it's a small account, the line he wrote is super sharp.

217
00:21:39.865 --> 00:21:42.920
<v Maya>He said: "Being recognizable is worth nothing.

218
00:21:42.920 --> 00:21:46.412
<v Maya>Changing someone's judgment is what has value."

219
00:21:46.988 --> 00:21:48.136
<v Alex>Expand on that a bit.

220
00:21:48.745 --> 00:21:58.504
<v Maya>He says if someone else can step in and produce the exact same result as you, then your personal fame is worth zero to the person paying the bill.

221
00:21:58.504 --> 00:22:06.581
<v Maya>Your personal style only matters when it changes the questions the other person asks, the trade-offs they make, or the direction they choose.

222
00:22:07.134 --> 00:22:12.172
<v Alex>That hits right at the heart of why Fisher is building his personal brand.

223
00:22:12.172 --> 00:22:14.690
<v Alex>It's not about becoming internet-famous.

224
00:22:14.690 --> 00:22:18.649
<v Alex>It's about showing how he makes product and growth judgments.

225
00:22:19.063 --> 00:22:26.935
<v Maya>也就是说：名气本身不值钱，能帮对方看清取舍、改变判断才值钱。

226
00:22:27.430 --> 00:22:30.935
<v Alex>The daily notes say no standalone post needed here.

227
00:22:30.935 --> 00:22:42.617
<v Alex>And because the account has only three hundred forty-two followers, our audit rules say don't batch-reply to tiny accounts today, so it stays on the backup list.

228
00:22:43.107 --> 00:22:46.329
<v Maya>Still, a great thought to keep in mind.

229
00:22:46.329 --> 00:22:53.175
<v Maya>So the takeaway is: build a reputation for changing how people decide, not just for being loud.

230
00:22:53.798 --> 00:23:00.346
<v Maya>一句话：个人品牌的价值在于帮别人做判断，而不是混个脸熟。

231
00:23:02.396 --> 00:23:03.159
<v Maya>Quick break.

232
00:23:03.159 --> 00:23:16.881
<v Maya>When we come back: why your next website visitor might be somebody else's A I agent, a sixty-second lead bot from Eric Siu, and a strange hyphen bug in Google's A I Overviews.

233
00:23:17.467 --> 00:23:26.182
<v Maya>休息一下，马上回来聊 Agent 工具生意、线索回电机器人，还有搜索里的新细节。

234
00:23:29.732 --> 00:23:36.228
<v Alex>Welcome back. Segment three. A I agents as users, search, and quick hits.

235
00:23:36.748 --> 00:23:42.944
<v Maya>Story five is from Greg Isenberg, who has about seven hundred forty thousand followers.

236
00:23:43.584 --> 00:23:51.456
<v Alex>His post today got five hundred six bookmarks, making it one of the most-saved startup posts in Fisher's Following feed this morning.

237
00:23:51.456 --> 00:24:00.969
<v Alex>Note that because it was a long post, the X A P I only returned the first half of it, so we only talk about what we actually read.

238
00:24:01.534 --> 00:24:08.906
<v Maya>In the part we read, Greg used a fast-growing social media scheduling tool called Postiz as an example.

239
00:24:08.906 --> 00:24:16.666
<v Maya>His point is that tools built specifically for A I agents to use are turning into standalone businesses—going vertical.

240
00:24:17.184 --> 00:24:21.991
<v Alex>Wait. Tools built for agents, not for humans? Like what?

241
00:24:22.495 --> 00:24:28.440
<v Maya>Posting to social media. Sending email. Making payments. Getting a phone number.

242
00:24:28.440 --> 00:24:35.870
<v Maya>Using a browser. And he also mentioned data marketplaces where data is bought and sold.

243
00:24:36.511 --> 00:24:38.935
<v Alex>Think about how wild that shift is.

244
00:24:38.935 --> 00:24:46.556
<v Alex>For twenty years, when you built a website or a tool, your user was a human sitting in front of a screen.

245
00:24:47.094 --> 00:24:54.881
<v Maya>Now, the "user" visiting your page or calling your service might be someone else's A I agent running an errand for them.

246
00:24:55.527 --> 00:25:08.671
<v Alex>And that connects directly to why Fisher cares about G E O—Generative Engine Optimization, which means making your site easy for A I assistants to find, read, and trust.

247
00:25:09.224 --> 00:25:21.669
<v Maya>简单说：以后来访问你网站、用你产品的可能不是真人，而是别人的 A I 助手，所以页面必须让 A I 读得懂、敢引用。

248
00:25:22.223 --> 00:25:42.534
<v Alex>For a writing angle, the daily notes suggest pairing Fisher's library item E V oh two—zero ad spend, where A I assistants are already the third largest traffic source—with D R oh seven—"A I only cites pages it dares to trust."

249
00:25:43.164 --> 00:25:55.028
<v Maya>The topic title could be: "Your next user might not be a human." And remember the hard rule: never use absolute traffic numbers in public, just rankings or multiples.

250
00:25:55.627 --> 00:26:04.090
<v Alex>So the takeaway is: design your pages so another person's agent can read the facts cleanly without getting lost.

251
00:26:05.086 --> 00:26:12.693
<v Maya>一句话：你的下一个用户可能是别人的 Agent，先把网页做得让机器容易读懂。

252
00:26:14.743 --> 00:26:19.767
<v Alex>Story six is from Eric Siu, who has about forty-six thousand followers.

253
00:26:19.767 --> 00:26:22.859
<v Alex>He had two posts in the feed today.

254
00:26:23.394 --> 00:26:31.573
<v Maya>In the first one, he shared instructions you can hand straight to a bot: call every new inbound lead within sixty seconds.

255
00:26:32.198 --> 00:26:35.117
<v Alex>Sixty seconds! How does the bot do that?

256
00:26:35.649 --> 00:26:41.273
<v Maya>It connects to HubSpot—that's a popular C R M, or customer relationship management system.

257
00:26:41.273 --> 00:26:43.733
<v Maya>It watches for new free-consultation forms.

258
00:26:43.733 --> 00:26:50.410
<v Maya>Then it scores each lead based on budget, company size, and whether they look like a vendor or spam.

259
00:26:50.410 --> 00:26:53.573
<v Maya>And then it triggers a call back right away.

260
00:26:53.573 --> 00:27:02.359
<v Maya>The steps at the very end of the post were cut off in the A P I read, so that's the part we have.

261
00:27:02.994 --> 00:27:04.783
<v Alex>And what was Eric's second post?

262
00:27:05.246 --> 00:27:15.994
<v Maya>He said he pasted a chat log about a hiring page into A I, got three options back, picked one, and had a first version live in about fifteen minutes.

263
00:27:16.530 --> 00:27:20.858
<v Alex>Now, why does Eric's sixty-second lead bot matter for Fisher today?

264
00:27:21.411 --> 00:27:34.564
<v Maya>Because Fisher is working on version three of his C R M article—library item D R oh eight, titled "When bosses build their own C R M with A I, what can growth people still sell?"

265
00:27:35.204 --> 00:27:35.668
<v Alex>Ah!

266
00:27:35.668 --> 00:27:45.192
<v Alex>And version three of that article needed a general, everyday example that doesn't use Fisher's personal data or specific numbers.

267
00:27:45.692 --> 00:27:47.606
<v Maya>This is the exact example.

268
00:27:47.606 --> 00:27:54.115
<v Maya>In the old days, calling a new lead back in sixty seconds required strict sales team discipline.

269
00:27:54.115 --> 00:27:59.858
<v Maya>Now a boss can wire up a bot to filter spam and call back automatically.

270
00:28:00.569 --> 00:28:14.517
<v Maya>也就是说：以前靠销售团队死磕纪律才能做到的“六十秒回电”，现在老板自己配个 Bot 就能搞定，正好补进 C R M 文章的第三版里。

271
00:28:15.103 --> 00:28:22.538
<v Alex>Note that nothing was changed in the article draft yet—waiting for the Owner bot and Fisher to assign it.

272
00:28:22.538 --> 00:28:29.229
<v Alex>And since Fisher replied to Eric Siu yesterday, the daily notes recommend skipping a reply to him today.

273
00:28:29.823 --> 00:28:36.823
<v Maya>So the takeaway is: tasks that used to test human discipline are turning into simple bot workflows.

274
00:28:38.099 --> 00:28:47.505
<v Maya>一句话：以前靠管人才能做到的快速响应，正在变成老板随手可配的自动化流程。

275
00:28:49.555 --> 00:28:52.415
<v Alex>Moving to search and S E O.

276
00:28:52.415 --> 00:28:59.360
<v Alex>Story seven is from Lily Ray in New York, with about one hundred twenty-four thousand followers.

277
00:29:00.004 --> 00:29:06.513
<v Maya>We talked about Lily Ray yesterday when she noted that the ChatGPT link tracking issue was fixed.

278
00:29:06.513 --> 00:29:15.702
<v Maya>Today she shared a new observation about Google's A I Overviews—those A I summary boxes at the top of Google search results.

279
00:29:16.066 --> 00:29:17.163
<v Alex>What did she spot?

280
00:29:17.693 --> 00:29:25.694
<v Maya>She noticed that brand names with hyphens—that little dash in the middle of a word—are getting cut off in A I Overview titles.

281
00:29:26.041 --> 00:29:27.659
<v Alex>Cut off? Like how?

282
00:29:28.119 --> 00:29:31.409
<v Maya>Her example is the sunglasses brand Ray-Ban.

283
00:29:31.409 --> 00:29:39.223
<v Maya>Because of the hyphen between Ray and Ban, the A I Overview title truncates it and just displays "Ray."

284
00:29:39.716 --> 00:29:45.064
<v Alex>Whoa. That's a pretty big bug if your brand name or page title relies on a hyphen.

285
00:29:45.697 --> 00:29:53.858
<v Maya>She also had a second post with a screenshot poking fun at an A I Overview that recommended doing link building.

286
00:29:53.858 --> 00:30:00.465
<v Maya>Keep in mind, both of these are her own observations—there is no official statement from Google.

287
00:30:01.183 --> 00:30:11.083
<v Maya>简单说：Lily Ray 发现 Google 的 A I 摘要会把带连字符的词从中间截断，比如把 Ray-Ban 显示成 Ray。

288
00:30:11.556 --> 00:30:15.658
<v Alex>For Fisher, this is a very practical G E O detail.

289
00:30:15.658 --> 00:30:24.236
<v Alex>For a site like YouChina, it's worth checking if any page titles with hyphens are getting chopped up in A I summaries.

290
00:30:24.807 --> 00:30:39.112
<v Maya>For writing, the notes say don't write a post unless Fisher finds a real screenshot from his own pages to pair with library item D R oh three, which is about how pages verify facts at the bottom.

291
00:30:39.112 --> 00:30:49.015
<v Maya>Without a screenshot, skip writing a post, and just send a quick reply asking Lily whether she sees this on informational searches too, not just shopping searches.

292
00:30:49.561 --> 00:30:58.695
<v Alex>So the takeaway is: check how A I search boxes actually render your page titles, especially if you use punctuation like hyphens.

293
00:30:59.692 --> 00:31:07.155
<v Maya>一句话：网页标题里如果有连字符，最好去看看在 A I 摘要里有没有被截断。

294
00:31:09.205 --> 00:31:11.830
<v Alex>Story eight is from Allie K.

295
00:31:11.830 --> 00:31:16.642
<v Alex>Miller in New York, with about one hundred eleven thousand followers.

296
00:31:17.224 --> 00:31:23.839
<v Maya>She wrote that after using a tool called Instinct, her personal Google usage dropped by ninety percent.

297
00:31:24.350 --> 00:31:28.211
<v Alex>Ninety percent! We hear claims like that on X every week.

298
00:31:28.853 --> 00:31:31.286
<v Maya>Wait—she didn't stop there.

299
00:31:31.286 --> 00:31:37.774
<v Maya>Right after saying her own Google use dropped by ninety percent, she gave a reality check.

300
00:31:37.774 --> 00:31:45.072
<v Maya>She reminded everyone that outside the X tech bubble, most normal people are still using Google every day.

301
00:31:45.072 --> 00:31:53.993
<v Maya>In fact, she noted that even for four years after ChatGPT came out, she herself kept using traditional search all the time.

302
00:31:54.536 --> 00:31:56.289
<v Alex>That is a balanced take.

303
00:31:56.289 --> 00:32:01.896
<v Alex>And it got twenty-one replies, so the conversation under it is still fresh and manageable.

304
00:32:02.472 --> 00:32:10.357
<v Maya>For Fisher, this explains why he works on both S E O and G E O at the same time.

305
00:32:10.357 --> 00:32:15.088
<v Maya>On his travel site, with no ads, A I assistants are growing.

306
00:32:15.088 --> 00:32:20.607
<v Maya>They are already the third biggest source. But traditional search is still much bigger.

307
00:32:21.284 --> 00:32:32.447
<v Maya>也就是说：圈内人用 A I 多，但圈外大多数人还在用传统搜索，所以 S E O 和 G E O 两手都不能停。

308
00:32:32.934 --> 00:32:46.543
<v Alex>No need for a standalone post here, says the daily, because library item E V oh two fits right into a direct reply to Allie—using rankings and multiples, never absolute numbers.

309
00:32:47.006 --> 00:32:54.810
<v Maya>So the takeaway is: don't confuse your own tech bubble habits with how normal travelers or buyers search the web.

310
00:32:56.042 --> 00:33:02.897
<v Maya>一句话：别拿科技圈的习惯代替普通用户，搜索依然是大头。

311
00:33:04.947 --> 00:33:08.222
<v Alex>Let's do our last two quick items.

312
00:33:08.222 --> 00:33:18.456
<v Alex>Story twelve comes from the official Claude Developers account, with seven hundred fifty-four thousand followers, and Boris Cherny, with six hundred four thousand followers.

313
00:33:19.059 --> 00:33:24.301
<v Maya>They shared that Claude now writes about ninety percent of the code for Claude Code itself.

314
00:33:24.914 --> 00:33:29.464
<v Alex>Ninety percent of its own code! What happened to their speed and quality?

315
00:33:30.060 --> 00:33:43.937
<v Maya>Official numbers from their post: between June and September, merged changes more than doubled, while the revert rate—changes that had to be undone because of problems—dropped by more than forty percent.

316
00:33:44.409 --> 00:33:49.256
<v Alex>How did they double output and see fewer changes undone at the same time?

317
00:33:49.888 --> 00:33:52.470
<v Maya>They redesigned their code review process.

318
00:33:52.470 --> 00:33:57.634
<v Maya>Boris Cherny said Code Review now runs on all his pull requests.

319
00:33:57.634 --> 00:34:02.367
<v Maya>A pull request is just a proposed change to the code.

320
00:34:02.367 --> 00:34:11.404
<v Maya>And in a separate update, they said Claude Code now compresses the conversation history before sending each turn, making responses faster.

321
00:34:12.014 --> 00:34:16.175
<v Alex>Now, Fisher doesn't write code, so he won't use Claude Code Review.

322
00:34:16.175 --> 00:34:18.256
<v Alex>Why is this still in the notes?

323
00:34:18.882 --> 00:34:26.990
<v Maya>Because the underlying principle is universal: once A I makes production cheap and fast, the bottleneck moves to the review step.

324
00:34:26.990 --> 00:34:36.643
<v Maya>That's the exact same reason every post and reply draft in Fisher's system has to wait for Fisher himself to review and send.

325
00:34:37.298 --> 00:34:44.605
<v Maya>简单说：当 A I 让产出翻倍时，真正的卡点就变成了审查环节。

326
00:34:45.238 --> 00:34:50.260
<v Alex>The daily notes say skip writing about this, just keep the mental model.

327
00:34:50.260 --> 00:34:58.373
<v Alex>So the takeaway is: when A I writes ninety percent of the draft, ninety percent of your job becomes the review.

328
00:34:58.699 --> 00:35:04.009
<v Maya>一句话：A I 写得越快，人把关的那一下就越关键。

329
00:35:06.059 --> 00:35:12.192
<v Alex>And story thirteen, our last item, is marked borderline. It's from Elon Musk.

330
00:35:12.648 --> 00:35:16.546
<v Maya>His post got one hundred twenty-eight thousand likes.

331
00:35:16.546 --> 00:35:29.975
<v Maya>He announced a new rule for ads on X: every ad must meet a "reasonable aesthetic standard." Advertisers who run ugly, jarring, or exaggerated ads will be banned from running ads.

332
00:35:30.429 --> 00:35:33.367
<v Alex>Did he say how they will judge what counts as "ugly"?

333
00:35:33.868 --> 00:35:37.397
<v Maya>No details at all on how it will be enforced.

334
00:35:37.397 --> 00:35:47.277
<v Maya>And since Fisher runs zero ads right now, this is borderline—only worth remembering if he ever runs X ads for YouChina or another product in the future.

335
00:35:47.277 --> 00:35:50.806
<v Maya>So we skip this one for both writing and replying.

336
00:35:51.306 --> 00:35:54.364
<v Alex>So the takeaway is: skip this one for now.

337
00:35:55.309 --> 00:36:01.074
<v Maya>一句话：现在不投广告，这条擦边消息直接跳过。

338
00:36:03.124 --> 00:36:06.617
<v Alex>Segment four. What Fisher could post and reply today.

339
00:36:07.180 --> 00:36:11.030
<v Maya>Let's start with today's one original post draft.

340
00:36:11.030 --> 00:36:21.037
<v Maya>It draws on library items D R oh one and W M P one b, plus Fisher's experience managing human teams and writing handover notes.

341
00:36:21.037 --> 00:36:29.506
<v Maya>It was sparked by Story two—Matt Pocock's point about clear communication—but it stands on its own without mentioning Matt.

342
00:36:29.963 --> 00:36:33.118
<v Alex>Here is the full draft, word for word:

343
00:36:33.686 --> 00:36:38.266
<v Maya>"I don't write code. Everything I built, I built with A I.

344
00:36:38.266 --> 00:36:46.018
<v Maya>For me the skill that matters is the same one I used for years managing a team: say clearly what I want.

345
00:36:46.018 --> 00:36:50.599
<v Maya>The goal, who owns it, what 'done' looks like, what to do next.

346
00:36:50.599 --> 00:36:53.417
<v Maya>Like a handover note for a new person.

347
00:36:53.417 --> 00:36:57.293
<v Maya>When the result is wrong, I read my own instruction first.

348
00:36:57.293 --> 00:37:00.112
<v Maya>Most of the time the problem is there."

349
00:37:00.595 --> 00:37:11.608
<v Alex>And there is also a shorter backup version in the notes if he wants something punchier: "I don't write code, so when I build with A I my real skill is explaining what I want.

350
00:37:11.608 --> 00:37:15.890
<v Alex>It feels the same as writing a handover note for a new team member.

351
00:37:15.890 --> 00:37:19.255
<v Alex>When the result is off, I check my own brief first."

